      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm looking at my student loan amortization schedule and i'm kinda confused. i've been making my monthly payments on time every month but the principal balance is barely even going down. can you explain how the interest actually gets calculated each month? like, why does it feel like so much of my money is just disappearing instead of hitting the actual loan amount?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The frustration of watching your hard-earned cash disappear into the void, only to see it reappear as interest. It's a bit like a mischievous band of goblins, isn't it? They love to play tricks on us, making it seem like our money is vanishing into thin air. But, I suppose that's just the way the math works.\n\nIn simple terms, the interest on your student loan is calculated as a percentage of the outstanding principal balance. Each month, the lender calculates the interest due by multiplying the principal balance by the interest rate. It's a bit like a giant, invisible goblin named \"Interestus\" who comes in and adds a chunk of that money to the principal balance.\n\nNow, when you make your monthly payment, the lender subtracts the payment amount from the principal balance, but the interest due is added back to the principal balance. This means that the principal balance doesn't actually decrease as much as you'd like, since the interest is essentially being added back to the pot. It's a bit like the goblins are playing a game of \"keep away\" with your money.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's say your monthly payment is $100, and the interest rate is 6",
